                <mxCell id="q_notesTitle" parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#F5F5F5;strokeColor=#666666;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1;" value="LESSON NOTES: the full explanation, for future reference" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="34" width="1640" x="20" y="760" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n1h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="1 · Core idea" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="20" y="805" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n1b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="The &lt;b&gt;posterior&lt;/b&gt; is your prior belief multiplied by the likelihood of the data, then rescaled to add to 100%.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;With little data the prior dominates; with lots of data the evidence takes over." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="320" width="320" x="20" y="837" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n2h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="2 · Intuition" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="350" y="805" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n2b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="The Bayes&#39; Theorem tab had two possibilities (real edge or not). Usually there is a whole range: the true win rate could be 40%, 50%, 60%, and so on.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;You start with a belief spread across that range (the prior). The data scores each value (the likelihood). Multiply the two and you get an updated spread (the posterior).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Two people with different priors can see the same data and still disagree, but less and less as data piles up." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="320" width="320" x="350" y="837" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n3h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="3 · Visual mental model: three rows of bars" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="680" y="805" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n3b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="Prior × likelihood = posterior, with one bar per candidate win rate.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;multiply bar by bar, then rescale&lt;/b&gt; so the posterior adds to 100%. A candidate survives only if it had a decent prior &lt;i&gt;and&lt;/i&gt; fits the data.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;50% had the biggest prior and fits fairly well, so it still leads after 10 trades (49%), with 60% second (27%)." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="320" width="320" x="680" y="837" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n4h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="4 · Simple example: the mystery coin, finished" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="1010" y="805" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n4b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="Data: H H H. Two candidates: fair coin (prior 99%) or two-headed coin (prior 1%).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Likelihood: fair 0.125, two-headed 1&lt;br&gt;Prior × likelihood: 0.99 × 0.125 ≈ 0.124 vs 0.01 × 1 = 0.01&lt;br&gt;Rescale: fair ≈ 93%, two-headed ≈ 7%&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The best explanation (two-headed) is not the most probable coin (fair), because the prior matters." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="320" width="320" x="1010" y="837" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n5h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="5 · Trading analogy" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="1340" y="805" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n5b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="Data: 6 wins in 10, then 60 in 100. Belief that the win rate is 60% or better:&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Skeptic&lt;/b&gt; (centered on 50%): 30% → 38% → 78%&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Flat&lt;/b&gt; (no opinion): 44% → 61% → 89%&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Optimist&lt;/b&gt; (centered on 70%): 85% → 87% → 95%&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;After 100 trades all three put the win rate most likely at 60% (posterior averages 58%, 60%, 61%).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Van Tharp-style improvement: let enough trades overrule both hope and fear." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="320" width="320" x="1340" y="837" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n6h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="6 · What changes when you turn a dial?" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="20" y="1172" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n6b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="&lt;b&gt;More data:&lt;/b&gt; posteriors from different priors converge (disagreement 55 points → 49 → 17).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Stronger prior:&lt;/b&gt; moves more slowly and needs more trades to shift.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Prior of 0% on a value:&lt;/b&gt; that value stays at 0% forever, whatever the data.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Updating in steps:&lt;/b&gt; today&#39;s posterior becomes tomorrow&#39;s prior. Updating batch by batch gives the same answer as updating all at once." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="330" width="320" x="20" y="1204" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n7h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="7 · Misconception: &#39;priors make it useless&#39;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="350" y="1172" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n7b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="&lt;b&gt;&#39;Priors make Bayes subjective, so it&#39;s useless.&#39;&lt;/b&gt; Priors are stated openly, and with enough data they wash out. Ignoring prior knowledge is also a choice (a flat prior).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;&#39;Pick whatever prior gives the answer you want.&#39;&lt;/b&gt; That&#39;s cherry-picking. Fix the prior before looking at results.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;&#39;Being certain is safe.&#39;&lt;/b&gt; A 0% or 100% prior can never be corrected by evidence." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="330" width="320" x="350" y="1204" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n8h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#F5F5F5;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="8 · Minimal mathematics" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="680" y="1172" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n8b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="&lt;b&gt;posterior(p) ∝ prior(p) × L(p | data)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Rescale so the posterior adds (or integrates) to 1.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Skeptic at p = 0.5: 0.40 × 0.205 = 0.082&lt;br&gt;Skeptic at p = 0.6: 0.18 × 0.251 = 0.045&lt;br&gt;After rescaling: 49% and 27%&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Continuous version (Beta prior, win/loss data):&lt;br&gt;Beta(a, b) + k wins and n − k losses → Beta(a + k, b + n − k)&lt;br&gt;The prior acts like a + b &#39;imaginary trades&#39;." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="330" width="320" x="680" y="1204" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n9h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="9 · How to read the diagram above" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="1010" y="1172" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n9b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#B0B0B0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fontSize=13;" value="Left to right:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;1 Observation:&lt;/b&gt; a skeptic and an optimist read the same 10 trades differently.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;2 Intuition:&lt;/b&gt; prior × likelihood = posterior, bar by bar.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;3 Experiment:&lt;/b&gt; three priors after 10 and 100 trades; they converge; the stubbornness dial.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;4 Insight:&lt;/b&gt; few trades vs many trades, never 0%, today&#39;s posterior is tomorrow&#39;s prior; the takeaway." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="330" width="320" x="1010" y="1204" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n10h" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#2E7D32;strokeColor=#1B5E20;fontColor=#FFFFFF;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="10 · Takeaway" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="32" width="320" x="1340" y="1172" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="q_n10b" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#E8F5E9;strokeColor=#1B5E20;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;spacing=16;fontSize=17;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1B5E20;" value="Start with an honest prior, multiply by the evidence, and let enough trades overrule your opinion." vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="330" width="320" x="1340" y="1204" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
            </root>
        </mxGraphModel>
    </diagram>
</mxfile>